00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.250
All right, let's talk about database leaks.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:47.850
Whether it be from a SQL injection attack,
a misconfigured server, or a disgruntled employee,

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.100
database leaks are bound to happen from time
to time.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.170
Not necessarily to your company, but to some
companies.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:01:00.739
So let's go through a hypothetical database
leak and see what happens if plaintext passwords

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:04.750
leaked,
versus hashed passwords, versus salted hashed

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:09.049
passwords, versus peppered salted hashed passwords.

00:01:09.049 --> 00:01:13.470
Each of these things provides a different,
extra, level of security that the previous

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:14.470
did not.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:17.250
All right, let's get started with plaintext
passwords.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.220
It's the simplest case and it provides the
least security for obvious reasons.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.380
Updating a password in plaintext just involves
taking the password from the user,

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.390
storing it in some kind of user object and
then sending that to the database to, you

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.369
know, update the record.

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:37.950
Verifying it is just comparing the inputted
password against the stored one in the database

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:41.020
and
one thing you can do is to use a compare digest,

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.860
which is a constant time compare, to avoid
things like timing attacks.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:48.880
Although if you're storing passwords at all
you've already messed up.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.780
In main here I've just got a dummy database
class that prints things out about the user

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.780
when you call the store method.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.710
Our users are just going to be a dataclass
with an email and a password,

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:02.620
and I'll simulate updating the password for
a user and then verifying the correct and

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.680
an incorrect password.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.280
In the event of a database leak, this is obviously
the worst case.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.660
The hacker gets every user's email and password
in plaintext.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:18.240
That means that they can use that email and
password to login as any user for this service,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.850
or they can try using those credentials to
log into other services.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:26.340
So if a hacker gets your Facebook password,
they may just try to log into your bank account

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:27.680
with that same information.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.181
Of course, you're supposed to use a unique
strong password for every service that you

00:02:32.181 --> 00:02:35.580
use, but who actually does that?

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.450
Let's see how we can improve the situation
by incorporating a hash function.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:44.040
In this version, we take in a hash function
and, instead of storing the raw password,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.890
we store the hashed version of the password.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.870
We additionally store the name of the hash
function that we used so that in the future,

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.970
if we ever want to migrate to a different
hash function, we can tell which function

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:57.740
was used to hash the password.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:01.930
Verifying the password then just comes down
to looking up the hash function that we used,

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:06.410
and then hashing the password with the given
hash function and comparing against the one

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:07.860
we have stored.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.450
It's also important to note here that for
all these examples I'm actually doing some

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.210
basic 64 encoding.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:17.180
This is very common in practice and it makes
it a lot easier to read when we print things

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.180
out.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.790
It's not super important,
but I do also want to note that the dollar

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.610
sign is not used in base 64 encoding.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:27.410
That makes it safe to use as a separator for
the name and hash,

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:31.900
and that's why we can just split the password
like this to recover those things.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:37.700
Okay so let's get to taking a look at what
the hacker sees when a database of hashed

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.210
passwords is leaked.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:43.110
So here's what the hacker would see if hashed
passwords were leaked.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.480
They can't see the passwords directly, so
they do have to do at least some work,

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:52.960
but they can recognize and break common passwords
because these hashes could be precomputed.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.360
They can also guess passwords offline for
those that aren't precomputed since they know

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.100
which hash function was used.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.590
This is a big win since the server won't ban
them for too many wrong attempts,

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:08.580
potentially allowing them to break weak passwords,
even if they haven't been precomputed.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.110
Also if they are able to crack a password
that multiple users have used,

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:16.370
they crack the password for everyone that
used that password all at once.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:20.680
They can also see if any two people have the
same password since those two passwords would

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.880
have the same hash,
even if the hacker isn't able to identify

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:27.240
what the password is from this data.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.040
This is a lot better situation than plaintext
passwords being leaked,

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:34.480
but, at this point, you should probably assume
that any one of your users that had a weak

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.490
password is compromised.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.640
So how can we improve this by using salts?

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.630
Well what is a salt anyway?

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:46.930
A salt is just a little bit of extra randomness
that we sprinkle in with a user's password.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.760
A salt should be unique for each user.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.570
You can see here that, instead of just hashing
the password directly,

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:56.290
we hash the salt together with the password.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:05:01.510
Then, when we store the password, we actually
stored the name, the salt, and the hashed

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:02.510
password.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:08.190
When we verify, we recover the salt from the
user's stored password and then use that together

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:11.930
with the inputted password,
hash it, and use that to compare against the

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.750
hashed password that was stored for the user.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:18.780
So here's what the hacker sees when salted
hashed passwords are leaked.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.280
Of course, they can identify the hashing algorithm
that's used and, because we put the salt in

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.130
there,
they also get the salt out.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:27.630
Of course, then there's the hash.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:32.740
But, if the salt is right there, what's the
purpose of using it?

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:35.110
The hacker just gets it for free...

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:40.340
Well the big win here is that hackers can
no longer recognize hashes of weak passwords

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:43.830
because throwing those salts in there made
them all unique.

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.370
Precomputed hashes become effectively worthless.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:51.520
A hacker can still guess passwords offline
and check to see if they're correct since

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.050
the salt is known,
that was part of the information that was

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:55.560
leaked in the database.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.860
This allows them to potentially break weak
passwords.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:05.210
But if the hacker does spend the time to crack
a weak password, it only cracks it for that

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:06.210
one person.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:10.770
It doesn't crack it for everyone that shared
the same password.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:15.830
They also can no longer tell if two people
have the same password by looking at the hash.

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:19.440
Okay then, what's a pepper and how does it
differ from a salt?

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.770
Well, a pepper is similar to a salt in that
it's some randomness that you sprinkle in

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:27.640
with the password,
but its primary difference is that a pepper

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:31.610
is specifically something that you do not
store in the database.

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:36.389
A salt was stored in the database with the
password, but a pepper is stored somewhere

00:06:36.389 --> 00:06:37.389
else.

00:06:37.389 --> 00:06:41.510
It's stored, perhaps, in your application
code, or in a secure memory enclave.

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:46.200
Because it's not stored in the database, it's
something that a hacker doesn't see even if

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.980
the database is leaked.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:52.520
Additionally, it's typical to only have one
pepper for your entire application.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.340
Since we can't store the pepper in the database,
it wouldn't make sense to have one for each

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:59.490
user because where would we store that information?

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:03.240
The way that you use a pepper, though, is
pretty similar to the way that you use a salt.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.750
You just sprinkle it in with the salt and
the password at hash time, and then when you

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:10.161
want to look it up,
you still get the salt from the hashed password

00:07:10.161 --> 00:07:14.030
that was stored in the database,
but you get the pepper from wherever you get

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.300
it, stored in some secure memory enclave.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:20.960
So what does using a pepper in addition to
the salt and hashing buy us?

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:26.600
Well, the hacker can still see the hash function
and salt, but the pepper isn't there because

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:28.090
it wasn't stored in the database.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.950
The hacker doesn't even know a pepper is involved.

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:36.400
And because the hacker doesn't know the pepper,
they can no longer guess weak password offline.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.830
The hacker would need to simultaneously leak
the database and the application code to get

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.540
any useful information out of the database
leak,

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:48.660
at which point it would be similar to the
situation where we were just using hashed

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:51.280
and salted passwords.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.080
The only downside to using a pepper is that,
since no database is used,

00:07:55.080 --> 00:08:00.080
it may be harder to ensure that every server
has access to this pepper.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.590
Because of these benefits, I think the peppers
are going to start picking up a lot more steam

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:04.590
in the future.

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:09.520
But, currently most libraries don't support
peppers, but they do support salts.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.580
I'd like to see that start to change in the
future, but right now that's just how it is.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:17.280
Because you really shouldn't be writing your
own authentication systems,

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.270
you should just be using open-source well-proven
ones, this probably means that you won't be

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:26.580
able to use peppers in the near future because
they don't support it yet.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:31.210
But I will at least link to a best-practices
document so that you can at least see what

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:36.200
you should be doing in the modern day
and how you should be storing passwords securely

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:41.180
using the tools and projects and libraries
that are available to us right now.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:45.430
So that's all I've got on hashing, salting,
and peppering your passwords, I hope you learned

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:46.490
a little bit.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:51.450
If you enjoyed the video, please don't forget
